---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
---
<Base title="About" description="How Learning Crumbs is made: questions from a parent and child, research checked against sources, and only freely licensed images." nav="about">
  <Breadcrumbs trail={[{ name: 'About' }]} />
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>About Learning Crumbs</h1>
    <p>
      My son once asked whether I used to write on a typewriter. To him, the past is one flat place where I might as well have been
      chiselling stone tablets. Answering him properly took me from correction fluid to keyboards, to printing, to paper, to papyrus.
      This site is that walk, written down, so anyone can follow it.
    </p>
    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <p>
      The site is laid out like a museum. Each <strong>gallery</strong> begins with a real question. Each <strong>exhibit</strong> has a
      label, a short text board, and a list of sources. At the bottom of every exhibit, a few <strong>crumbs</strong> lead to related
      exhibits, often in a different gallery. Follow them and you will end up somewhere you did not expect.
    </p>
    <h2>How we curate</h2>
    <p>
      Every exhibit is written fresh in our own words. We check the facts against reliable sources and list them at the foot of the page,
      so you can read further and judge for yourself. Where historians disagree, or a popular story is probably a legend, we say so.
    </p>
    <h2 id="images">Images</h2>
    <p>
      We only use images that are in the public domain or released under open licences, from places such as the Smithsonian, The Met,
      the Library of Congress and Wikimedia Commons. Each one carries its credit and licence. Until an exhibit has a suitable image,
      it shows a lettered plate instead.
    </p>
    <p>
      Occasionally no suitable historical image is free to use. Then we may use an illustration made with AI, drawn in a style that is
      clearly an illustration rather than a photograph, and always labelled "AI-generated illustration" in its caption. These never
      stand in for real historical evidence.
    </p>
    <h2>Spotted a mistake?</h2>
    <p>Knowledge keeps changing, and so will this site. If something here is wrong or out of date, please tell us.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
